Lewis should stop rolling once his current earnings are such that the expected value of rolling again is $0$. The reason for this is that as Lewis earnings get larger and larger, the marginal gain of making another roll starts to get outweighed by the chance that he loses everything if he rolls an $8$. Thus, every further roll after this point will incur a negative expected value, and so Lewis should stop rolling. 

$$$$

Given that Lewis currently has $\$v$, the expected value of a roll is,
\begin{align*}
    E[\text{Roll]} &= \frac{1}{8}E[ \text{Roll] \mid \text{Roll 8}} + \frac{7}{8}E[ \text{Roll] \mid \text{Roll 1 - 7}} \\
    &= \frac{1}{8}(-v) + \frac{7}{8} \cdot \frac{28}{7} 
\end{align*}
This is because if he rolls an $8$, which occurs with probability $\frac{1}{7}$, he loses his current earnings which is $\$x$. If he rolls a $1 - 7$, which occurs with probability $\frac{7}{8}$, then his expected earnings from the roll is $\frac{28}{7}$. The reason for this is that, given that he rolled a number in $1 - 7$, there is a $\frac{1}{7}$ chance that each number occurs, so that $E[ \text{Roll] | \text{Roll 1 - 7}} = \frac{1}{7}(1 + 2 + ... + 7) = \frac{28}{7}$.

Setting $E[\text{Roll]}$ equal to $0$ and solving for $v$ yields $v = 28$, so Lewis should stop rolling once his total earnings is $28$.

Our strategy for this question will be to calculate the probability that Rupert wins as a function of $k$, and then find the $k$ that maximizes this function.

$$$$

Let $W$ be the event that Rupert wins. To determine $P(W)$ we will count the number of permutations of cards that meet out criteria. If Rupert chooses $k$, three things must occur. The first is that in the first $k-1$, cards we must have exactly $2$ aces. This can happen in $\binom{4}{2}\binom{48}{k-3}(k-1)!$ ways, since there are $\binom{4}{2}$ to choose the $2$ aces that appear in the first $k-1$ cards, $\binom{48}{k-3}$ to choose the remaining $k-3$ cards from the available $48$, and then $(k-1)!$ ways to order them all. The second thing that must occur is that the $k$th card must be an ace. Since we already picked $2$ aces to go into the first $k-1$ cards, there are $2$ remaining aces for us to pick in the $k$th spot, which gives us $2$ choices. Finally, the remaining $52 - k$ cards can be ordered in $(52 - k)!$ ways.

$$$$

Since every ordering of cards is equally likely, and since there are $52!$ total ways to order the cards, the probability that Rupert wins is,
\begin{align*}
    P(W) &= \frac{ \binom{4}{2}\binom{48}{k-3}(k-1)!2(52-k)!  }{52!} \\
    &= \frac{ 2\binom{4}{2} \frac{48!}{(k-3)!(48 - (k-3))!} (k-1)!(52-k)!  }{52!} \\
    &= \frac{ 2\binom{4}{2} 48! (k-1)!(52-k)! }{ (k-3)!(51-k)!52!} \\
    &= \frac{ 12 (k-1)(k-2)(52-k) }{52 \cdot 51 \cdot 50 \cdot 49} \\
    &= \frac{ -12k^{3} + 660k^{2} - 1896k + 1248 }{52 \cdot 51 \cdot 50 \cdot 49}
\end{align*}

Since we want to find the optimal $k$, we will take the derivative and set it equal to $0$, and then solve for $k$. The derivative is,

\begin{align*}
    \frac{d}{dk} \frac{ -12k^{3} + 660k^{2} - 1896k + 1248 }{52 \cdot 51 \cdot 50 \cdot 49} &= \frac{ -36k^{2} + 1320k - 1896}{52 \cdot 51 \cdot 50 \cdot 49} \\
    & \rightarrow -36k^{2} + 1320 - 1896 = 0 \\
    & \rightarrow k = \frac{55}{3} \pm \frac{\sqrt{2551}}{3} \\
    & \rightarrow k \approx 1.5 \text{ or } 35.17
\end{align*}

Note that clearly we can't have $k = 1.5$, since $k$ must clearly be greater than $2$ (otherwise, we can't have $2$ aces to the left of $k$). We also need $k$ to be an integer (we can't pick the $35.17$th card). If we plug $35$ and $36$ into our expression for $P(W)$, we see that when $k = 35$ we get $P(W) \approx 0.03523$ and when $k = 36$ we get $P(W) \approx 0.03516$. Thus, the optimal $k$ is $k = 35$.

We note that there are other ways in which we could have come up with the expression for $P(W)$. Keeping in mind the conditions needed for Rupert to win, we instead could find $P(W)$ by noting that out of the $k-1$ spaces before the $k$th card, we need to choose $2$ of them to be filled with an ace, which can occur in $\binom{k-1}{2}$ ways. The $k$th cards needs to be an ace, and we need one ace to fill this singular spot, which can occur in $1$ way. Then, for the remaining $52 - k$ spaces, there will be one space that needs to have an ace placed in it, which can occur in $\binom{52-k}{1} = (52-k)$ ways. Finally, the $4$ aces can be ordered in $4!$ ways, and the other $48$ cards can be ordered in $48!$ ways. Thus,
\begin{equation*}
    P(W) = \frac{ \binom{k-1}{2}(52-k)4!48!}{52!}
\end{equation*}

After some algebra, this can be shown to be equivalent to the initial expression we derived for $P(W)$.

To optimize our expected earnings, we will decide to only roll the second dice if doing so provides a benefit (in expectation). Since the expected value of the $6-$sided die is $3.5$, and the expected value of the $8-$sided die is $4.5$, we should decide to reroll the first die if we roll anything below a $5$. If we roll a $5$ or $6$, we should keep the result of the first dice, since we expect to only get $4.5$ (on average) if we decide to roll the $8-$sided die. 

$$$$

To find the expected value of this strategy, let $R$ be the value we roll from the $6-$sided die, and let $V$ denote our payout. We then have

\begin{align*}
    E[V] &= P(R \leq 4)E[V \mid R \leq 4] + P(R > 4)E[ V \mid R > 4] \\
    &= \frac{4}{6}(4.5) + \frac{2}{6}\left(\frac{6 + 5}{2}\right) \\
    &= \frac{29}{6}
\end{align*}

This follows from the fact that if we roll less than or equal to a $4$ on the first roll, we will roll the $8-$sided die which has an expected value of $4.5$, and if we roll a value greater than $4$, then we must've rolled either a $5$ or a $6$.

