You and your friend are playing Mario Kart. Each turn, either you or your friend gets to select the map. If you select the map, you have probability $p_1$ of winning the turn. If your friend selects the map, you have probability $p_2$ of winning the turn. Consider the following two possible conventions for map selection:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $\textbf{Method A:}$ Alternating every turn
    \item $\textbf{Method B:}$ The player selecting continues to select until they lose
\end{itemize}

You get to select the map of the first turn. Each time someone wins, they receive a point. The first person to reach $n$ points wins the tournament. Assuming you know both $p_1$ and $p_2$, under an optimal strategy, which convention would you choose when $n = 10$ to maximize your probability of winning?
